<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · Dance · c. 1819 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">La Cucaracha</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A folk song about a cockroach that cannot walk; in Mexico's revolution, soldiers on every side made up new verses to tease their enemies.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Spanish soldiers and singers; later Mexican revolutionaries, soldiers and civilians of all factions</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Spain and Mexico</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>origins obscure; mentioned in an 1819 Mexican novel; verses from the Hispano-Moroccan War (1859–1860); most known verses from the Mexican Revolution (1910–c. 1920)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march · Dance</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; verses anonymous, many from 1859–1920)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-cucaracha">La Cucaracha</a>&quot; (&quot;The Cockroach&quot;) is a folk song whose refrain tells of a cockroach that has lost two of its legs and can no longer walk properly. Its origins are obscure and Spanish. Because the refrain names no event, it cannot be dated, but its verses were made up anew for each time and place and so can be roughly dated by the events they mention. One of the earliest written references to the song is in the Mexican writer José Joaquín Fernández de Lizardi's novel La Quijotita y su Prima (1819). In his Cantos Populares Españoles (1883), Francisco Rodríguez Marín recorded verses about the Hispano-Moroccan War of 1859–1860, probably made up by Spanish troops on campaign to keep up their spirits; other early verses refer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlist-wars">Carlist Wars</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-french-intervention-in-mexico">French intervention in Mexico</a> in 1861.</p><p>The song's great period came in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>, from 1910 to about 1920, when both rebel and government forces invented political verses for it, and most of the verses known today were written. They referred to battles, leaders and the hardship the war brought to civilians, often with hidden meanings. The best-known verses today are two of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pancho-villa">Pancho Villa</a>'s soldiers, aimed at President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoriano-huerta">Victoriano Huerta</a>: in them the cockroach stands for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huerta">Huerta</a>, who was known as a heavy drinker and was held a traitor for his part in the death of the revolutionary president Francisco Madero. Huerta's own followers sang verses against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venustiano-carranza">Venustiano Carranza</a>, Emiliano Zapata's followers had theirs, and civilians, many of them sympathetic to Zapata, sang verses complaining of the war's hardships or naming the factions without taking sides.</p><p>&quot;Cucaracha&quot; was also one of the nicknames, with soldaderas, Adelitas and Juanas, for the women who fought in or followed Mexican armies, a role traced back to before the Spanish conquest. Soldiers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a>'s army sang of a soldadera who wanted money for the bullfights; for Villa's men, as the historian Elizabeth Salas writes, the cockroach wanted money for alcohol and marijuana. Salas notes that, unlike the corridos about Villa or Zapata, none of the well-known songs about soldaderas give real names, so few verses tell truly of women in battle or in the camps.</p><p>Hundreds of other verses exist, old and new, passed on mostly by ear. The modern song has been adapted to the Mexican corrido, and its melody is known worldwide.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A light, mocking dance song: the refrain laughs at a cockroach that can no longer walk, and the verses, made up anew in each era, turn that joke on rival armies, politicians, the hardships of war or daily life. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a> the cockroach became a figure of ridicule for an enemy leader.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Spanish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Spanish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">La cucaracha, la cucaracha</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The cockroach, the cockroach</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">ya no puede caminar;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>can no longer walk,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">porque no tiene, porque le falta</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>because it has not, because it lacks</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">la patita principal.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>its main little leg.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">La cucaracha, la cucaracha</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The cockroach, the cockroach</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">ya no puede caminar;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>can no longer walk,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">porque no tiene, porque le falta</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>because it has not, because it lacks</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">marihuana que fumar.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>marijuana to smoke.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional: the older chorus, then the best-known chorus of the Mexican Revolution; as quoted in the Spanish Wikipedia's article. Text from Wikipedia (<a href="https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_cucaracha" target="_blank" rel="noopener">La cucaracha</a>); public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (2)</summary><ul><li>There is no fixed text: singers fit new verses to the chorus, and its last line changes with them. The first chorus is the older one; the second is the best known of the Mexican Revolution.</li><li>Satirical verses of the revolution aimed it at Victoriano Huerta and at Venustiano Carranza's troops; they vary from singer to singer and are not printed here.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
